"base_model_detail" = "LoRA is an additive model. It requires a base model and then fine-tuning from there. A good base model can reduce the number of steps required and have higher aestheticity for the final result.";

"output_lora_name_detail" = "The name for the LoRA that will display in the LoRA list. We also save training related information, such as hyperparameters and training dataset captions with this name. If you use the same name, we will be able to recover these information.";

"trigger_word_detail" = "The trigger word helps to fill the caption with the trigger word and gives the LoRA a good starting point. You should chooe a trigger word that doesn't carry a particular strong meaning to the model otherwise it might erase the original concept from the model.";

"network_dim_detail" = "\"Network Dim\" determines the LoRA network capacity. A larger value would require more data to train but might capture more nuances of the training data.";

"network_scale_detail" = "This is \"Network Alpha\" / \"Network Dim\" in other trainers. It determines the initial influence from randomized weights in LoRA network. Smaller scale might help to stablize the training.";

"training_image_size_detail" = "The resolution when training the network. We center crop and scale the provided image to this resolution. Make sure the training data is larger or equal to this resolution otherwise the network might learn upscaling artifacts.";

"training_steps_detail" = "How many training steps to perform. This has to be modified in conjunction with learning rate to make good model. Lower learning rate, more capacity requires more training steps.";

"save_every_n_steps_detail" = "This allows us to save LoRAs at every n steps such that we can avoid overcook a model.";

"warmup_steps_detail" = "This controls at which step we will use the full learning rate for training. Warmup steps help to stablize the training dynamics earlier by avoiding overweigh on early examples.";

"gradient_accumulation_steps_detail" = "This controls when we perform a network update. Accumulating more gradients before update helps the network to get more statistics about where to go. You should scale the learning rate along with this value (i.e. 2x accumulation steps, 2x learning rate).";

"cotrain_text_model_detail" = "Whether to fine-tune the text encoder. For SD v1 / v2 models, fine-tuning the text encoder would help to popularize a concept quicker into the model. This is often not neccessary when fine-tuning SDXL.";

"text_model_learning_rate_detail" = "The learning rate for fine-tuning the text encoder.";

"noise_offset_detail" = "\"Offset noise\" introduces extra offset to the noise distribution. This helps fine-tuning the model to better represent dark / bright scenarios rather than falling into medium exposure always.";

"denoising_schedule_detail" = "This controls at which stage of denoising process we focus on fine-tuning. For example, you can train a LoRA that helps to \"refine\" an image by just focusing on the denoising schedule between 70% to 100%. Or a LoRA that helps to get good composition at schedule between 0% to 50%.";

